  "worked_solution": "We can classify the equilibrium solutions of an autonomous ODE ${y}^{\u2032}=f(y)$ by examining its phase line.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *sink* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *toward* the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *source* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *away* from the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *node* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line point in the same direction.\n\nFirst, we need to find the equilibrium solutions. So, we solve $f(y)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(y) & =0 \\\\ 2+y-{y}^{2} & =0 \\\\ -({y}^{2}-y-2) & =0 \\\\ -(y+1)(y-2) & =0 \\\\ y & =-1,2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equilibrium solutions are $y=-1$ and $y=2.$ \n\nNow, we analyze the sign of $f(y)$ using a sign table, as follows:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-1)$ | $(-1,2)$ | $(2,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-(y+1)$ | $+$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $y-2$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| $f(y)$ | $-$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n\nSo, we have that $f(y)$ is negative for $y\\in (-\\infty ,-1)\\cup (2,\\infty ),$ and positive for $y\\in (-1,2).$\n\nWe can now draw the phase line for this equation.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071359/assets/9230d847daa518edfaad43f6552a48c2b3cac9523a1e9ecad8624851080e00ea.png)\n\nFinally, we classify the equilibrium solutions:\n\n- $y=-1$ is a source because the nearby solutions diverge away from this point.\n\n- $y=2$ is a sink because the nearby solutions converge toward this point.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$y=-1$ is a source, and $y=2$ is a sink\".",
